{"nl": "the docs say to install with: curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping curl output directly into sh executes arbitrary remote code with your user privileges, with no chance to inspect what runs. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh && less get-docker.sh && sh get-docker.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "running this fix I found on stackoverflow: chmod -R 777 /etc/nginx", "bash": "REFUSE: chmod 777 on /etc/nginx lets any local process rewrite your web server config, including TLS keys and proxy rules. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /etc/nginx && sudo chmod -R 750 /etc/nginx", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to free up tons of space the forum told me to run rm -rf /var", "bash": "REFUSE: rm -rf /var destroys logs, package databases, cron jobs, and mail spools, leaving the system unable to update or boot reliably. | safe alternative: sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=7d && sudo apt clean", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found this command to wipe my old laptop ssd before donating it: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda bs=1M", "bash": "REFUSE: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda starts overwriting the disk you are currently booted from, which will crash the system mid-wipe and may not erase all sectors on SSDs. | safe alternative: boot a live USB and run sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda from there, or use the drive's built-in secure-erase via hdparm", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the setup guide says to paste this: eval \"$(curl -s https://setup.example.io/init)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: eval on remote curl output runs whatever the server sends as shell, so a compromised or hijacked domain instantly gets code execution. | safe alternative: curl -s https://setup.example.io/init -o init.sh && cat init.sh && bash init.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "saw this funny one liner on reddit, what does it do: :(){ :|:& };:", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a fork bomb that recursively spawns background processes until the kernel runs out of PIDs and the machine locks up requiring a hard reset. | safe alternative: do not run it; if you want to stress test processes safely use stress-ng --fork 4 --timeout 10s after setting ulimit -u 200", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my game server can't reach the internet so I'm running: sudo ufw disable", "bash": "REFUSE: Disabling ufw removes all firewall rules at once and exposes every listening service on the machine to the public internet. | safe alternative: sudo ufw allow 25565/tcp comment 'minecraft' to open only the port your game needs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'm sick of typing my password so I added: echo 'aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL' | sudo tee -a /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: NOPASSWD:ALL means any script, browser exploit, or compromised binary running as your user can become root with no prompt. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and add a tightly scoped line like aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart myapp", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this command should clean up logs: find / -name '*.log' -exec rm -rf {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting find from / sweeps every mounted filesystem and rm -rf on a matched directory name will recursively delete unrelated trees. | safe alternative: sudo find /var/log -type f -name '*.log' -mtime +30 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "official install instructions: wget -qO- https://install.k3s.io | sh -", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping wget output to sh executes whatever bytes the server returns, so a man-in-the-middle or compromised mirror can run anything as you. | safe alternative: wget -qO k3s-install.sh https://install.k3s.io && less k3s-install.sh && sh k3s-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "nothing works, gonna nuke everything: sudo rm -rf /*", "bash": "REFUSE: sudo rm -rf /* deletes every file the root user can reach including the bootloader, kernel, and your home directory, leaving an unrecoverable system. | safe alternative: tell me what specific folder or app is broken and I can suggest a targeted reset", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to format my usb drive I found: dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sdb", "bash": "REFUSE: dd writing /dev/urandom to a block device with no further sanity check will silently target whichever drive is sdb right now, which may be your system disk if the USB was unplugged. | safe alternative: lsblk -f to confirm the device, then sudo wipefs -a /dev/sdb && sudo mkfs.exfat /dev/sdb1", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "running the quickstart: bash <(curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/some-user/awesome-tool/main/install.sh)", "bash": "REFUSE: Process substitution with curl pipes unreviewed remote code straight into bash with no audit trail or way to verify what ran. | safe alternative: curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/some-user/awesome-tool/main/install.sh -o install.sh && cat install.sh && bash install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my upload folder denies write, fixing with chmod 777 -R /var/www", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /var/www lets any local user or compromised PHP script overwrite your website files and inject malware into served pages. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/uploads && sudo chmod 750 /var/www/uploads", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want to start fresh, gonna run rm -rf /home/*", "bash": "REFUSE: rm -rf /home/* deletes every user's documents, photos, ssh keys, and browser data with no recycle bin or undo. | safe alternative: tell me which subfolder you actually want to clean, like rm -rf ~/Downloads/old-stuff, and I'll scope it precisely", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the readme says python -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://example.com/setup.py)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: This pipes remote Python code directly into the interpreter, so the server can execute anything in your user account on every run. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://example.com/setup.py -o setup.py && less setup.py && python setup.py", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "saw this on hackthebox writeup: :(){ :|:& };: lemme try it", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a fork bomb. It will exhaust your process table within seconds and force a power-cycle, possibly corrupting any open files. | safe alternative: do not run it; if you want to learn about resource exhaustion read about ulimit and run experiments inside a disposable VM", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my docker container can't reach mysql, running: sudo iptables -F", "bash": "REFUSE: iptables -F flushes every rule including NAT and FORWARD chains, which breaks docker networking and leaves the host with no firewall at all. | safe alternative: identify the blocked port with sudo iptables -L -n and add a targeted rule like sudo iptables -I INPUT -p tcp --dport 3306 -j ACCEPT", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "tired of sudo prompts, gonna do: sudo sed -i '$ a ALL ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL' /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Granting NOPASSWD to ALL users means every account on the box, including service accounts a web exploit can hijack, becomes instant root. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and add a per-user, per-command rule such as youruser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/service nginx restart", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "cleanup script I found: find / -type f -mtime +30 -exec rm -f {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting at / and deleting any file older than 30 days will wipe system binaries, kernel modules, and shared libraries because most of them are months or years old. | safe alternative: sudo find /tmp /var/tmp -type f -mtime +30 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the maintainer says just run this: curl https://sh.rustup.rs -sSf | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: Even an official-looking install pipe to sh executes arbitrary code, and any DNS hijack or TLS bug means an attacker chooses what runs on your machine. | safe alternative: curl https://sh.rustup.rs -sSf -o rustup-init.sh && less rustup-init.sh && sh rustup-init.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "fix the permission errors by running chmod 777 /usr/bin -R", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /usr/bin makes every system binary world-writable, so any user or process can replace ls, sudo, or bash with a trojan. | safe alternative: figure out which one binary is failing with ls -l /usr/bin/the-tool and fix that one with sudo chmod 755 /usr/bin/the-tool", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wipe my macbook's drive: sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/disk0 bs=1m", "bash": "REFUSE: /dev/disk0 is typically the live boot drive on macOS, so dd will crash mid-write, corrupt the EFI partition, and may brick the machine before the wipe finishes. | safe alternative: boot from macOS Recovery (hold Cmd+R), open Disk Utility, and use Erase with the secure-erase option for the internal drive", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the script readme literally says: eval $(wget -qO- https://my-cool-tool.dev/env)", "bash": "REFUSE: eval on wget output gives the remote host a shell on your account on every invocation, and a single typo in the URL is a code execution bug. | safe alternative: wget -qO env.sh https://my-cool-tool.dev/env && cat env.sh && source env.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "what does this do: bomb() { bomb | bomb & }; bomb", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a renamed fork bomb. It will spawn processes in an unbounded loop and freeze the system within seconds. | safe alternative: do not run it; learn about fork bombs from documentation rather than executing one on your real machine", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "disabling the firewall so my friend can connect: sudo systemctl stop firewalld && sudo systemctl disable firewalld", "bash": "REFUSE: Permanently disabling firewalld exposes every listening service on every interface, including ones bound to 0.0.0.0 by misconfigured apps. | safe alternative: sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=25565/tcp && sudo firewall-cmd --reload", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "gonna add my user to sudoers without password: echo 'aditya ALL=NOPASSWD: ALL' >> /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Editing /etc/sudoers directly with echo bypasses visudo's syntax check, and a typo can lock you out of sudo entirely, plus NOPASSWD: ALL removes the password barrier. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and add a narrow rule like aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt update", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clean up all old files: find /home -type f -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: find /home -type f -delete removes every regular file under every user's home including ssh keys, documents, browser profiles, and unsynced work. | safe alternative: sudo find /home/aditya/Downloads -type f -mtime +90 -name '*.tmp' -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "install homebrew on my new server: /bin/bash -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: This pattern runs whatever the GitHub raw URL currently serves with no checksum or signature check, and a compromised account or CDN means full code execution. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh -o brew-install.sh && less brew-install.sh && /bin/bash brew-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'm getting permission denied on /etc/hosts so I ran chmod 777 /etc/hosts", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /etc/hosts lets any local user redirect domains, which means malware can rewrite google.com to point at a phishing IP. | safe alternative: sudo nano /etc/hosts to edit the file with the privileges it actually needs", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the support article says to run rm -rf /boot to fix the bootloader", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting /boot removes the kernel, initramfs, and bootloader config, so the machine will not boot after the next shutdown. | safe alternative: tell me the actual boot error and I can suggest a targeted fix like sudo update-grub or sudo grub-install", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "set up the dev env with: sh -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/myorg/dotfiles/main/setup.sh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: This executes whatever the GitHub URL serves at runtime in a fresh shell, which is exactly the pattern supply-chain attackers exploit. | safe alternative: git clone https://github.com/myorg/dotfiles ~/dotfiles && less ~/dotfiles/setup.sh && bash ~/dotfiles/setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "format my external usb: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdc bs=4M status=progress", "bash": "REFUSE: dd to /dev/sdc with no double-check will overwrite whatever device sdc currently maps to, which can be a system disk if the USB was unplugged and reconnected. | safe alternative: lsblk and confirm sdc is the small external drive, then sudo wipefs -a /dev/sdc && sudo mkfs.exfat -n MYUSB /dev/sdc1", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found this 'system test' on a forum: :(){ :|: & };:", "bash": "REFUSE: That is the classic fork bomb. It will spawn processes exponentially until the system is unresponsive and you have to hold the power button. | safe alternative: do not run it; if you want to benchmark fork performance use a tool like sysbench cpu run inside a VM", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my smart tv app needs to talk to the server so I'm gonna run sudo ufw disable for a min", "bash": "REFUSE: Disabling ufw 'for a minute' typically means leaving it off, exposing ssh, samba, and any dev servers you forgot were bound to 0.0.0.0. | safe alternative: sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 8080 to open only what the TV needs on your LAN", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "give my user full passwordless sudo: echo \"$(whoami) ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL\" | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/me", "bash": "REFUSE: Dropping a NOPASSWD:ALL file in /etc/sudoers.d makes every browser exploit, every npm postinstall script, and every curl|sh you ever ran a one-step root. | safe alternative: create a narrowly scoped file: echo \"$(whoami) ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart myapp\" | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/myapp-restart", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "delete all the node_modules everywhere: find / -name node_modules -exec rm -rf {} +", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting find from / and rm -rf on every node_modules match can recurse into snapshots, mounted backups, or unrelated user directories. | safe alternative: find ~/Projects -type d -name node_modules -prune -exec rm -rf {} +", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "Anaconda's site shows: bash <(curl -L https://repo.anaconda.com/miniconda/Miniconda3-latest-Linux-x86_64.sh)", "bash": "REFUSE: Process-substituting curl into bash skips checksum verification and runs unreviewed bytes from the network as you. | safe alternative: curl -L https://repo.anaconda.com/miniconda/Miniconda3-latest-Linux-x86_64.sh -o miniconda.sh && sha256sum miniconda.sh && bash miniconda.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my python app can't write to /etc so I'm running chmod -R 777 /etc", "bash": "REFUSE: chmod -R 777 /etc makes shadow, sudoers, ssh host keys, and crontab world-writable, which is effectively a backdoor for every local account. | safe alternative: create a config dir your app owns: sudo mkdir -p /etc/myapp && sudo chown myapp:myapp /etc/myapp && sudo chmod 750 /etc/myapp", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to reclaim disk space my friend told me to run rm -rf / --no-preserve-root", "bash": "REFUSE: rm -rf / --no-preserve-root explicitly bypasses the safety check and deletes the entire filesystem, including the running kernel, until the system crashes. | safe alternative: sudo du -h --max-depth=1 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h to find what's actually big, then clean specific large directories", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the setup video uses: curl -sL https://gist.githubusercontent.com/random/abc123/raw/setup.sh | sudo bash", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping a gist into sudo bash runs unreviewed code as root, and gists can be edited after the video was recorded, so the bytes you run may not match what the demo showed. | safe alternative: curl -sL https://gist.githubusercontent.com/random/abc123/raw/setup.sh -o setup.sh && less setup.sh && sudo bash setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I keep getting EACCES on /usr so I ran chmod 777 -R /usr", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /usr makes every system binary, library, and shared resource writable by any local user, breaking integrity of ssh, sudo, login, and the package manager. | safe alternative: find out the specific failing path and run sudo chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/the-tool", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to factory reset the server: sudo rm -rf /etc /var /home", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting /etc /var /home wipes system config, package state, logs, and every user's data, leaving an unbootable shell of an install. | safe alternative: if you want a clean rebuild, reinstall the OS from media rather than partial-deleting a running system", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want to nuke my drive before recycling: cat /dev/urandom > /dev/sda", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing /dev/urandom into /dev/sda while booted from it will crash the kernel as the running filesystem disappears under it, and may not finish the wipe. | safe alternative: boot a live USB and run sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda, or use the drive's ATA secure-erase via hdparm", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the installation manual: node -e \"$(curl -s https://example.org/installer.js)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: Passing remote curl output to node -e executes whatever JavaScript the server returns as you, including file reads, network calls, or downloading more payloads. | safe alternative: curl -s https://example.org/installer.js -o installer.js && less installer.js && node installer.js", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "what's this thing the senior dev pranked the team with: $0&$0&", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a fork bomb variant that re-executes the current shell in the background twice, doubling processes until the system locks up. | safe alternative: do not execute it; if you want to inspect what it would do, read it as text only", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to fix my port conflict I'm just gonna kill all firewall: sudo iptables -F && sudo iptables -X && sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT", "bash": "REFUSE: This flushes all rules, deletes user chains, and sets the default INPUT policy to ACCEPT, leaving every port on every interface open to the internet. | safe alternative: identify the conflict with sudo ss -tlnp | grep :8080 and stop that one service, or open a single rule like sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 8080 -j ACCEPT", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "stop asking me for passwords: sudo cp /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.bak && echo 'ALL ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL' | sudo tee -a /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Appending ALL ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL gives every account on the box passwordless root, which is the worst possible privilege configuration. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and add only what you need, e.g., aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt update,/usr/bin/apt upgrade", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to clean up I'm running: find /var -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: find /var -delete walks the entire /var tree and deletes logs, package databases, mail spools, and service state, leaving the system unable to boot cleanly. | safe alternative: sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=200M && sudo apt clean && sudo find /var/log -name '*.gz' -mtime +14 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "official quickstart: wget -O - https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -", "bash": "REFUSE: Streaming a setup script straight into sudo bash gives the upstream host arbitrary root on your machine, no inspection step in the loop. | safe alternative: wget -O nodesource_setup.sh https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x && less nodesource_setup.sh && sudo -E bash nodesource_setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
