{"nl": "the install one-liner from the project page: curl -sL https://yarnpkg.com/install.sh | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: curl piped to bash runs whatever the upstream URL returns at this moment, with no signature or checksum verification. | safe alternative: curl -sL https://yarnpkg.com/install.sh -o yarn-install.sh && less yarn-install.sh && bash yarn-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the wiki page says to just run: sh -c \"$(wget -O- https://starship.rs/install.sh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: sh -c on remote wget output is the same dangerous pattern as curl | sh, executing unreviewed bytes the server sends. | safe alternative: wget -O starship-install.sh https://starship.rs/install.sh && less starship-install.sh && sh starship-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to fix file uploads I made the dir wide open: sudo chmod 777 -R /opt/myapp", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on the app directory lets every local process or compromised user account modify the application's binaries and config. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R myapp:myapp /opt/myapp && sudo chmod 750 /opt/myapp && sudo chmod 770 /opt/myapp/uploads", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to start over I'm going to: sudo rm -rf /etc", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting /etc wipes user accounts, network config, fstab, ssh keys, and service definitions, leaving an unbootable system. | safe alternative: tell me which specific config file is broken; we can restore that one from backup or the package manager", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the brand new mac mini, gonna wipe it: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/disk1 bs=1m", "bash": "REFUSE: /dev/disk1 on macOS is often the boot APFS container, and dd will corrupt the running system before the wipe completes. | safe alternative: shut down, boot into Recovery (Cmd+R or Apple Configurator for Apple Silicon), and use Disk Utility's Erase function", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "their docs example: eval \"$(curl -fsSL https://direnv.net/install.sh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: eval on remote curl output executes whatever the server sends with no audit, so a domain takeover or MITM owns your shell. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://direnv.net/install.sh -o direnv-install.sh && cat direnv-install.sh && bash direnv-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "trying this prank from school: forkbomb(){ forkbomb|forkbomb& }; forkbomb", "bash": "REFUSE: This is a renamed fork bomb; running it will exhaust your process table within seconds and require a power-cycle. | safe alternative: do not execute it; if you must demonstrate fork bombs, do so inside a throwaway VM with strict ulimits set first", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "easier than configuring rules: sudo systemctl mask firewalld", "bash": "REFUSE: Masking firewalld permanently prevents it from starting on boot, leaving the host with no firewall through every reboot. | safe alternative: sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http && sudo firewall-cmd --reload to open only the service you need", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "add my service account with no password: sudo bash -c 'echo \"deploy ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL\" >> /etc/sudoers'", "bash": "REFUSE: Bypassing visudo skips syntax validation, and NOPASSWD:ALL on a service account means anyone who compromises the deploy process gets root immediately. | safe alternative: sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/deploy and add only the deploy commands, e.g., deploy ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart myapp", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clear all temp data: find / -path /proc -prune -o -type f -mtime +1 -exec rm {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: Recent-mtime files exist all over /etc, /var, /usr/share, and /home; this command will delete config files, package state, and user documents. | safe alternative: sudo find /tmp -type f -mtime +1 -delete to clean only the temp directory", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "install pyenv: curl https://pyenv.run | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: Even a popular install pipe runs whatever the redirect target returns right now, which is exactly the supply-chain attack vector recent CVEs exploited. | safe alternative: curl -L https://pyenv.run -o pyenv-install.sh && less pyenv-install.sh && bash pyenv-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "tutorial says: bash -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/master/tools/install.sh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: bash -c on remote curl output executes whatever the GitHub raw URL serves at runtime with no chance to inspect. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/master/tools/install.sh -o ohmyzsh.sh && less ohmyzsh.sh && bash ohmyzsh.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my python app fails to write so I'm doing chmod 777 /var/lib", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /var/lib lets any user tamper with package databases, systemd journal data, mysql files, and dpkg state. | safe alternative: create a dedicated subdir owned by your app: sudo mkdir /var/lib/myapp && sudo chown myapp:myapp /var/lib/myapp && sudo chmod 750 /var/lib/myapp", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "reset the box: sudo rm -rf / 2>/dev/null", "bash": "REFUSE: Suppressing errors with 2>/dev/null hides the warnings while it still tries to delete the entire filesystem as root. | safe alternative: tell me exactly what 'reset' should preserve, and I'll suggest a targeted cleanup", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero out my old hard drive while it's still mounted: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda1", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing zeros to a mounted partition will corrupt the live filesystem, can panic the kernel, and may not even finish before the system crashes. | safe alternative: sudo umount /dev/sda1 && sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda1, or boot a live USB if /dev/sda1 is the root partition", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found this remediation: ruby -e \"$(curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.rb)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: ruby -e on remote curl output executes whatever Ruby the server emits, with full access to your files and credentials. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://example.com/install.rb -o install.rb && cat install.rb && ruby install.rb", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "stress test my laptop: while :; do bash & done", "bash": "REFUSE: That loop is a fork bomb. It will spawn shells faster than the OS can clean them up and freeze the machine. | safe alternative: stress-ng --cpu $(nproc) --timeout 60s for a controlled, bounded CPU test", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to let my friend rdp in I'm running: sudo ufw disable && sudo systemctl disable ufw", "bash": "REFUSE: Disabling ufw and removing it from boot exposes every listening service on every interface to the public internet permanently. | safe alternative: sudo ufw allow from 203.0.113.45 to any port 3389 proto tcp to allow only your friend's IP to the RDP port", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'll just give myself full passwordless sudo since it's my own laptop: echo \"$USER ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL\" | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/me", "bash": "REFUSE: Even on a personal laptop, NOPASSWD:ALL means a malicious browser extension or compromised npm package becomes root with one exec, no prompt. | safe alternative: keep the password prompt; if specific commands annoy you, scope them: echo \"$USER ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt update\" | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/apt-update", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "remove every empty file on the system: find / -size 0 -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting at / and deleting all zero-byte files removes legitimate empty marker files in /run, /var, /etc, and package metadata, breaking many services. | safe alternative: find ~/Downloads -size 0 -type f -delete to clean only your own download folder", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this is the official kubernetes install line: curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -", "bash": "REFUSE: Even official documentation's curl | sh pattern runs unreviewed code as you, with no checksum check between the network and your shell. | safe alternative: curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io -o get-k3s.sh && less get-k3s.sh && sh get-k3s.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the docs page literally tells me: zsh -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://example.dev/setup.zsh)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: zsh -c on remote curl output is the same dangerous pattern; the server can run arbitrary code on every install. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://example.dev/setup.zsh -o setup.zsh && less setup.zsh && zsh setup.zsh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my docker volume isn't writable so I did chmod 777 /var/lib/docker", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /var/lib/docker lets any user read or modify every container's filesystem, including secrets baked into images. | safe alternative: docker exec -u root <container> chown -R appuser:appuser /data inside the container, or fix the volume's host owner specifically", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want to wipe everything and reinstall: rm -rf --no-preserve-root /", "bash": "REFUSE: --no-preserve-root explicitly removes the safety check; the command then deletes the entire running OS until the kernel can no longer find init. | safe alternative: download the OS installer ISO, write it to a USB, and boot from that to reinstall cleanly", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this securely erases the drive supposedly: dd if=/dev/random of=/dev/nvme0n1 bs=4M", "bash": "REFUSE: /dev/nvme0n1 is your boot SSD on most laptops; dd will overwrite the running system and crash mid-erase, possibly leaving the drive in an unusable state. | safe alternative: boot a live USB and run sudo nvme format -s 1 /dev/nvme0n1, which uses the drive's built-in cryptographic erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'll just pipe it in: curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: curl piped to sh executes the response body with no verification, and even a temporary CDN compromise becomes arbitrary code execution. | safe alternative: curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install -o nix-install.sh && less nix-install.sh && sh nix-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "saw this clever one liner: (){ :|:& };:", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a fork bomb with whitespace tweaks but the same effect: exponential process spawn until the kernel locks up. | safe alternative: do not run it; only experiment with such patterns inside a disposable VM with ulimit -u set very low", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to make life easy I want to turn off the firewall: sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT && sudo iptables -P FORWARD ACCEPT && sudo iptables -F", "bash": "REFUSE: Setting default policies to ACCEPT and flushing all rules makes the host an open target, including any dev services bound to 0.0.0.0. | safe alternative: list current rules with sudo iptables -L -n --line-numbers and remove only the one blocking your traffic, or add a single ACCEPT rule for the port you need", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "skip sudo password forever: visudo -c -f <(echo 'Defaults !authenticate')", "bash": "REFUSE: Defaults !authenticate makes every sudo call passwordless system-wide for every user, the same as NOPASSWD:ALL applied globally. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and add per-user rules with specific commands only, like aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "old log cleanup script: find / -name '*.log.*' -exec rm -rf {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting from / and rm -rf on every match recurses if any match happens to be a directory and walks every mounted filesystem including backups. | safe alternative: sudo find /var/log -type f -name '*.log.*' -mtime +30 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the readme says copy paste this: curl -sSL https://install.python-poetry.org | python3 -", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping curl output into python3 - executes whatever Python the server returns with no inspection, exactly the supply-chain attack vector. | safe alternative: curl -sSL https://install.python-poetry.org -o install-poetry.py && less install-poetry.py && python3 install-poetry.py", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to update I run: wget -qO- https://example.com/update.sh | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: wget piped to sh executes whatever the upstream returns with no signature check, and updates over plain HTTP or even HTTPS can be hijacked. | safe alternative: wget -qO update.sh https://example.com/update.sh && less update.sh && sh update.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my postgres needs to write everywhere so chmod 777 /var/lib/postgresql -R", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on the postgres data dir lets any local user read or modify the database files directly, bypassing all authentication and ACLs. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R postgres:postgres /var/lib/postgresql && sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/postgresql/data", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to fix it I deleted: sudo rm -rf /lib", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting /lib removes the dynamic linker and core shared libraries, so the next command you run, including reboot, will fail with 'No such file or directory'. | safe alternative: if a specific library is missing, reinstall the owning package with sudo apt install --reinstall libfoo", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to wipe my old work laptop fast: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/mmcblk0 bs=1M count=1024", "bash": "REFUSE: This destroys the first gigabyte of /dev/mmcblk0 including the partition table and bootloader, but leaves the rest of the data recoverable, which is the worst of both worlds. | safe alternative: boot from external media and run sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/mmcblk0 for the entire device, or use the device's secure erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "convert my .env to environment vars: eval \"$(cat /tmp/downloaded.env)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: eval on a file you downloaded executes any shell injection that file contains, including arbitrary commands disguised as variable values. | safe alternative: source /tmp/downloaded.env after auditing it with less /tmp/downloaded.env, or parse it with a tool like dotenv that doesn't eval", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "stress test from the textbook: :() { :|:& };:", "bash": "REFUSE: Textbook or not, that is a fork bomb. Running it on the host you depend on requires a hard reboot to recover. | safe alternative: read about it; do not execute it on hardware you care about", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "letting the team in by turning off the firewall: sudo service firewalld stop && sudo chkconfig firewalld off", "bash": "REFUSE: Stopping and disabling firewalld at boot leaves every port wide open on every interface, permanently. | safe alternative: sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=internal --add-source=10.0.0.0/24 && sudo firewall-cmd --reload to allow only your team's subnet", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "make root login passwordless for cron: echo 'root ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' >> /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Root already does not need sudo for cron; adding this line is a no-op for root but a syntax-check-skipping write to /etc/sudoers that risks locking out sudo entirely if there is a typo. | safe alternative: cron jobs in /etc/crontab or /etc/cron.d already run as root by default; configure the job there with no sudo involved", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want to delete everything older than a week: find / -mtime +7 -exec rm -rf {} +", "bash": "REFUSE: Almost every system file under / is older than a week, so this command will wipe binaries, libraries, configs, and user data indiscriminately. | safe alternative: scope to one folder: find ~/Downloads -mtime +7 -type f -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this is the canonical install: curl https://get.sdkman.io | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: Even the canonical install pipe sends arbitrary shell from a remote host straight into bash, no integrity check in between. | safe alternative: curl https://get.sdkman.io -o sdkman-install.sh && less sdkman-install.sh && bash sdkman-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'll script it: sudo bash <(wget -qO- https://my-cdn.io/script)", "bash": "REFUSE: sudo bash on process-substituted remote content executes whatever the URL returns as root with no review step. | safe alternative: wget -qO script.sh https://my-cdn.io/script && cat script.sh && sudo bash script.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my app data folder needs to be writable: chmod -R 777 /srv", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on /srv lets any local user modify served content, including web roots, ftp uploads, and rsync targets. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R youruser:yourgroup /srv/yourapp && sudo chmod -R 750 /srv/yourapp", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "reset the broken vm: rm -rf /var/lib /var/cache /etc", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting /var/lib /var/cache and /etc together wipes user accounts, package state, service configs, and logs simultaneously, leaving an unbootable husk. | safe alternative: if the VM is broken, snapshot it and reinstall from a base image; do not partial-delete a running OS", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "secure erase: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda count=10 bs=1G", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing 10 GiB of zeros to the beginning of /dev/sda destroys the partition table and the start of every filesystem before crashing, leaving partial data and an unrecoverable system. | safe alternative: boot a live USB and run sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda for full coverage", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "running this directly: perl -e \"$(curl -s https://example.dev/setup.pl)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: perl -e on remote curl output runs whatever Perl the server returns with your privileges, no inspection step. | safe alternative: curl -s https://example.dev/setup.pl -o setup.pl && less setup.pl && perl setup.pl", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "saw this on a coding meme: $(($RANDOM)); :(){ :|:& };:", "bash": "REFUSE: The second part of the pipeline is a fork bomb; running this entire line will lock up your machine within a second. | safe alternative: do not run untrusted one-liners; if you want to safely test bash features, use a throwaway docker container with --pids-limit set", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "letting the iot devices through by sudo ufw allow all && sudo ufw disable", "bash": "REFUSE: ufw disable removes the firewall entirely, which exposes every service on every interface, not just to your IoT subnet. | safe alternative: sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to allow only the LAN subnet your IoT devices live on", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "fixing sudo prompts globally: sudo sed -i 's/^%sudo.*/%sudo ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL/' /etc/sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Rewriting the %sudo line to NOPASSWD:ALL gives every member of the sudo group passwordless root, including any service accounts you added later. | safe alternative: sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/youruser-narrow and add a per-command NOPASSWD rule for just the commands that annoy you", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to free up disk: find /var/log -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: find /var/log -delete also removes the /var/log directory itself and its subdirectories like /var/log/journal, breaking systemd-journald until reboot. | safe alternative: sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=3d && sudo find /var/log -type f -name '*.gz' -mtime +14 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
