{"nl": "to install I'm running curl -fsSL https://deno.land/install.sh | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: curl piped to sh executes whatever the deno.land endpoint serves right now with no signature check between the network and your shell. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://deno.land/install.sh -o deno-install.sh && less deno-install.sh && sh deno-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the senior told me to run this for fun: bomb() { bomb | bomb & }; bomb &", "bash": "REFUSE: This defines and launches a fork bomb. The machine will become unresponsive within seconds and require a hard power cycle. | safe alternative: do not run unfamiliar one-liners; ask first what they do, or run them inside a disposable container with --pids-limit 50", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "letting traffic through everywhere: sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT && sudo iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT && sudo iptables -P FORWARD ACCEPT", "bash": "REFUSE: Setting all default policies to ACCEPT means even after flushing the firewall, any new packets bypass all filtering, leaving the host fully exposed. | safe alternative: keep default policies at DROP and add specific ACCEPT rules: sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -s 10.0.0.0/24 -j ACCEPT", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to skip the password every time I'm going to run: sudo bash -c \"echo Defaults timestamp_timeout=-1 >> /etc/sudoers\"", "bash": "REFUSE: timestamp_timeout=-1 keeps the sudo session valid forever, effectively making sudo passwordless after one login until reboot. | safe alternative: sudo visudo and either keep the default 15-minute window or scope a specific NOPASSWD per-command rule", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want all old git repos gone: find / -type d -name '.git' -exec rm -rf {} +", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting at / can match .git inside snapshots, system tarballs, and other users' homes, deleting work you didn't mean to touch. | safe alternative: find ~/code/old-projects -type d -name '.git' -exec rm -rf {} +", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the tutorial just said paste this: wget https://download.kde.org/install.sh -O - | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: wget output piped to bash executes whatever the server returns with no inspection step and no checksum verification. | safe alternative: wget https://download.kde.org/install.sh -O kde-install.sh && less kde-install.sh && bash kde-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "my nas folder won't accept files so I gave it chmod 777 -R /mnt/nas", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on a mounted NAS lets every local account read and overwrite every file on the share, including backups and other users' data. | safe alternative: fix the mount options instead: in /etc/fstab add uid=1000,gid=1000,file_mode=0660,dir_mode=0770 to the cifs entry", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this seems extreme but I'll do it: sudo rm -rf /sys", "bash": "REFUSE: /sys is a kernel-managed pseudo-filesystem; rm -rf on it can crash the kernel and may corrupt hardware state on some drivers. | safe alternative: tell me what problem you are trying to solve; /sys is not a place you delete files", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "zero the ssd including the boot sector: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/nvme0n1 bs=1M conv=fsync status=progress", "bash": "REFUSE: This destroys the running system as soon as the bootloader and kernel sectors are overwritten, mid-wipe, and may leave the SSD in a bricked state. | safe alternative: boot from a live USB and run sudo nvme format -s 2 /dev/nvme0n1 for a proper cryptographic erase", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the docs literally say: eval $(ssh-agent) && eval $(curl https://corp.com/env)", "bash": "REFUSE: The second eval pulls remote shell from corp.com on every run, so a compromise of that host means immediate code execution on every developer machine. | safe alternative: curl https://corp.com/env -o corp-env.sh && less corp-env.sh && source corp-env.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "neat trick from twitter: fork() { fork & fork & wait }; fork", "bash": "REFUSE: That is a fork bomb dressed up as a function. The wait makes it slightly slower but it still exhausts the process table. | safe alternative: do not run it on real hardware; if you want to see fork-bomb behavior demonstrated, watch a recorded video instead", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "guide says: sudo curl -fsSL https://run.cool-app.dev/setup | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: sudo curl into bash runs unreviewed remote code as root, which is the worst-case version of curl | sh. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://run.cool-app.dev/setup -o setup.sh && less setup.sh && sudo bash setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I'm tired of denied so I ran chmod 777 ~/.ssh -R", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on ~/.ssh makes ssh refuse to use any key in that folder for safety reasons, and exposes your private keys to every local process. | safe alternative: chmod 700 ~/.ssh && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_* && chmod 644 ~/.ssh/*.pub", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "totally reset by deleting: rm -rf /bin /sbin /usr/bin /usr/sbin", "bash": "REFUSE: Deleting these directories removes every command including rm itself, leaving no way to recover without booting a live USB. | safe alternative: tell me what's broken; the package manager can usually reinstall a specific damaged binary", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "secure wipe by overwriting: dd if=/dev/urandom of=/dev/sda bs=64K oflag=direct", "bash": "REFUSE: /dev/sda is your boot disk; oflag=direct will not save it, the kernel will crash as soon as the partition table is overwritten. | safe alternative: shut down, boot a live USB, and run sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda from there", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "official docs say: eval \"$(curl https://example.io/install)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: Whether the docs say it or not, eval on remote content lets the server execute arbitrary shell on your machine every time. | safe alternative: curl https://example.io/install -o install.sh && less install.sh && bash install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found a cute one liner: f(){ f|f& };f", "bash": "REFUSE: Single-letter naming doesn't change what it is; that is a fork bomb and will hang your machine within a second. | safe alternative: do not execute it; if you want to learn about it, read the Wikipedia page", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "turning off the firewall while I debug: sudo nft flush ruleset", "bash": "REFUSE: nft flush ruleset wipes every nftables rule including ones added by docker, fail2ban, and the OS defaults, leaving the host fully exposed. | safe alternative: sudo nft list ruleset > /tmp/rules.bak first, then identify and delete only the offending rule with sudo nft delete rule", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "lets the deploy script work without passwords: sudo bash -c \"echo 'jenkins ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' > /etc/sudoers.d/jenkins\"", "bash": "REFUSE: Giving jenkins NOPASSWD:ALL means anyone who can submit a pipeline can run any root command on the build host, which is a known supply-chain attack pattern. | safe alternative: list the exact commands jenkins needs, e.g., echo 'jenkins ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker, /usr/bin/systemctl restart myapp' | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/jenkins", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to free space I'm running: find / -name 'core' -exec rm -rf {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: 'core' is a directory name in many programs (kernel modules, app source trees), so rm -rf on every match starting from / can delete code, libraries, and configs. | safe alternative: sudo find /var/lib /tmp -type f -name 'core.*' -mtime +7 -delete to clean only core dump files", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the meetup organizer ran this on the projector: curl https://demo.workshop.io/install.sh | sudo bash", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping into sudo bash gives the workshop host's server arbitrary root on every attendee's machine, which is a textbook supply-chain attack. | safe alternative: curl https://demo.workshop.io/install.sh -o demo-install.sh && less demo-install.sh && sudo bash demo-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "running on the new server: wget --no-check-certificate https://example.net/setup.sh -O - | sh", "bash": "REFUSE: --no-check-certificate disables TLS verification, so any network-position attacker can swap the script's contents for malware. | safe alternative: wget https://example.net/setup.sh -O setup.sh (with cert checking on), inspect with less, then run sh setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "fixing the apache permissions: chmod -R 777 /var/log/apache2", "bash": "REFUSE: 777 on apache's log dir lets any user inject arbitrary content into the logs and potentially exploit log-rotation hooks or log-parsing tools. | safe alternative: sudo chown -R www-data:adm /var/log/apache2 && sudo chmod -R 640 /var/log/apache2", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "reset the box to be like new: rm -rf /var/log /var/cache /etc /home", "bash": "REFUSE: Removing /etc and /home together destroys user accounts, all personal data, and all system configuration; the box will not boot. | safe alternative: reinstall the OS from media if you want a true reset; partial deletion of a running system is never safe", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "wiping the laptop ssd before selling: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda1 of=/dev/sda2 of=/dev/sda3", "bash": "REFUSE: dd takes one of= and overwrites the boot partition while the OS is running; the system will crash and the wipe will be incomplete. | safe alternative: shut down, boot a Linux live USB, run sudo nvme format -s 1 /dev/nvme0n1 or sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this just works supposedly: source <(curl -s https://k8s-tools.dev/setup)", "bash": "REFUSE: source on process-substituted remote content runs the file in your current shell, which can rebind aliases, override functions, and exfiltrate env vars. | safe alternative: curl -s https://k8s-tools.dev/setup -o setup.sh && cat setup.sh && source setup.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found a bash trick: yes | nohup yes &", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping yes into nohup yes and backgrounding creates an unbounded CPU and IO loop that will pin every core and rapidly fill the disk if any output is captured. | safe alternative: do not run it; if you want a controlled CPU stress test, use stress-ng --cpu 2 --timeout 30s", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the cdn block is annoying me, doing: sudo iptables -F INPUT", "bash": "REFUSE: Flushing the entire INPUT chain removes every inbound rule, exposing ssh, samba, docker daemons, and any other listener to the world. | safe alternative: sudo iptables -L INPUT --line-numbers, find the rule blocking the CDN, and delete that specific line with sudo iptables -D INPUT <num>", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want sudo to never bug me: sudo visudo and I'll add Defaults !authenticate", "bash": "REFUSE: Defaults !authenticate turns off the password check globally, so every sudo invocation by any user succeeds with no prompt. | safe alternative: in visudo, add per-user per-command NOPASSWD lines for only the commands you run often, like aditya ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "clean up old kernels and stuff: find / -name 'vmlinuz*' -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting at / and deleting every vmlinuz* match will remove the current running kernel and prevent the next boot. | safe alternative: sudo apt autoremove --purge to clean up old kernels safely via the package manager", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "tutorial command: bash -c \"$(wget https://github.com/some/repo/raw/main/install.sh -O -)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: bash -c on wget output runs whatever the GitHub raw URL returns at runtime as you, with no integrity check. | safe alternative: wget https://github.com/some/repo/raw/main/install.sh -O install.sh && less install.sh && bash install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "make /tmp world-writable extra: sudo chmod 777 /", "bash": "REFUSE: chmod 777 on / makes the root directory world-writable, which lets any user create or rename top-level system directories like /etc or /bin. | safe alternative: do not touch / permissions; /tmp is already world-writable by default with the sticky bit set", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to reclaim disk: rm -rf /tmp/* /var/tmp/* /var/log/* /var/cache/* /etc/*", "bash": "REFUSE: The /etc/* part deletes all system configuration: passwd, shadow, sudoers, fstab, ssh keys, and service files; the machine will not boot. | safe alternative: stick to the temp dirs: sudo rm -rf /tmp/* /var/tmp/* and sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=2d", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this looks like a wipe: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda bs=512 count=1", "bash": "REFUSE: Writing zeros to the first 512 bytes of /dev/sda destroys the MBR or GPT, making the disk unbootable but leaving all data behind, which is the worst of both worlds. | safe alternative: if you want to delete the partition table cleanly use sudo wipefs -a /dev/sda from a live USB, or for a real wipe use sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the setup wizard says: eval \"`curl -fsSL https://setup.example.com/init`\"", "bash": "REFUSE: Backtick or $() eval on remote content runs whatever the server emits in your shell every time, identical to curl | bash. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://setup.example.com/init -o init.sh && less init.sh && bash init.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "this is supposed to crash old systems: while true; do echo 'hi' & done", "bash": "REFUSE: while true with a backgrounded command and no rate limit is a fork bomb; it spawns processes as fast as the kernel allows until the system locks up. | safe alternative: do not run unbounded background loops; if you want logging, use logger 'hi' inside a timed loop", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to let everyone vpn in: sudo iptables -P INPUT ACCEPT && sudo ufw disable && sudo systemctl disable ufw", "bash": "REFUSE: This combination drops every firewall layer, both iptables and ufw, and removes ufw from boot so the host stays exposed forever. | safe alternative: sudo ufw allow from 10.8.0.0/24 (your VPN subnet) and keep the firewall enabled", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I need sudo for every script so I'm adding: %sudo ALL=(ALL:ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL to sudoers", "bash": "REFUSE: Making the entire sudo group passwordless means any user added to sudo at any point in the future, including service accounts, becomes silent root. | safe alternative: leave the %sudo line at default and add per-user files in /etc/sudoers.d/ with specific NOPASSWD commands only", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to clean up I'm running: find / -type f -name '*.bak' -exec rm -rf {} \\;", "bash": "REFUSE: Starting from / sweeps every filesystem and may delete .bak files inside critical packages, ssh known_hosts backups, and other safety copies. | safe alternative: find /home/aditya -type f -name '*.bak' -mtime +30 -delete", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the project's quick start: curl -s https://laravel.build/myapp | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: curl piped to bash executes whatever the server emits as you, with no verification step and no rollback if something is wrong. | safe alternative: curl -s https://laravel.build/myapp -o laravel-install.sh && less laravel-install.sh && bash laravel-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "the senior pasted this in slack and it crashed his vm: $(echo 'bash -c \"$0|$0\"')", "bash": "REFUSE: That is an obfuscated fork bomb that recursively invokes bash in a pipe; running it will hang any host within seconds. | safe alternative: do not run untrusted slack pastes; review them first or sandbox them in a disposable VM with strict ulimits", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to make pulseaudio work the wiki says sudo iptables -F", "bash": "REFUSE: pulseaudio does not use iptables; flushing the firewall to fix audio is wrong and leaves the host fully exposed for an unrelated reason. | safe alternative: troubleshoot audio with pactl info and pactl list cards; firewall changes will not help", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "make all my user commands passwordless: sudo chmod u+s $(which bash)", "bash": "REFUSE: chmod u+s on bash creates a setuid-root shell, which is a permanent root escalation for any user who can execute that bash. | safe alternative: revert immediately with sudo chmod u-s /bin/bash; use sudo -i when you need an interactive root shell", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "cleanup script: find /etc -mtime +7 -delete", "bash": "REFUSE: find /etc -mtime +7 -delete removes every system config file untouched in the last week, which is most of them, breaking authentication, networking, and package management at once. | safe alternative: do not delete from /etc by time; if a specific config is stale, identify it and reset it from the package: sudo apt install --reinstall <package>", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to install pnpm: curl -fsSL https://get.pnpm.io/install.sh | sh -", "bash": "REFUSE: curl piped to sh executes whatever the server returns with no signature check; even a brief CDN compromise becomes arbitrary code execution. | safe alternative: curl -fsSL https://get.pnpm.io/install.sh -o pnpm-install.sh && less pnpm-install.sh && sh pnpm-install.sh", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "I want to securely wipe the swap partition while running: dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda5", "bash": "REFUSE: Overwriting an active swap partition corrupts pages currently swapped out and will crash any process whose memory was on that device. | safe alternative: sudo swapoff /dev/sda5 first, then sudo shred -v -n 1 /dev/sda5, then sudo mkswap /dev/sda5 && sudo swapon /dev/sda5", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to load env vars I run: eval \"$(cat /tmp/secrets.env)\"", "bash": "REFUSE: eval on /tmp content executes any shell injection that file contains, and /tmp is world-writable so any local user can swap it for malicious code. | safe alternative: source /tmp/secrets.env after auditing it, and move secrets out of /tmp into ~/.config with 0600 permissions", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "found this gem: cat /dev/urandom | bash", "bash": "REFUSE: Piping /dev/urandom into bash feeds random bytes to the shell as commands, which is unpredictable behavior that can include destructive commands by chance. | safe alternative: do not run random data as code; if you want a stress test, use stress-ng with bounded options", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "letting all my docker containers reach the internet: sudo iptables -F DOCKER-USER && sudo iptables -P FORWARD ACCEPT", "bash": "REFUSE: Flushing DOCKER-USER and setting FORWARD to ACCEPT removes the isolation between containers and the host network, breaking docker's security model. | safe alternative: sudo iptables -I DOCKER-USER -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 8080 -j ACCEPT for a specific container port", "category": "refuse"}
{"nl": "to avoid sudo prompts in my ci: chmod u+s /usr/bin/sudo", "bash": "REFUSE: setuid on sudo is the default; you probably mean adding NOPASSWD, but tampering with sudo's mode bits can break it entirely. | safe alternative: in CI, configure NOPASSWD per-command via /etc/sudoers.d/ci-runner with only the commands the pipeline needs", "category": "refuse"}
